2026-06-30 15:08:30,197 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-999ba190-bb7d-4e6a-a423-1d4bb32d6098', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_BioID2_01_002.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_BioID2_03_014.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_BioID2_04_020.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_BioID_02_008.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_BioID_05_026.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_BioID_06_032.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_GFP_01_001.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_GFP_02_007.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_GFP_03_013.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_GFP_04_019.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_GFP_05_025.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_GFP_06_031.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_TTBK1_01_005.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_TTBK1_02_011.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_TTBK1_03_017.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_TTBK1_04_023.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_TTBK1_05_029.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_TTBK1_06_035.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_TTBK2_01_006.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_TTBK2_02_012.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_TTBK2_03_018.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_TTBK2_04_024.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_TTBK2_05_030.raw\nQP1_181026_BB1_S_BioID_TTBK2_06_036.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nIdentifying interactors through BioID2 in rat cortical neurons\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nUtilizing proximity labeling technolgy coupled to LC-MS/MS to identify TTBK1 and TTBK2 unique interactors in rat cortical neurons\n\n[METHODS]\nNeurons were treated after 2 days in vitro with TTBK1, TTBK2, or control GFP containing virus. Seven days following virus transduction, 20µM biotin was added to the neuron media and incubated for 16 hours. Neurons were harvested by first being washed with cold PBS and then lysed in lysis buffer (0.5M NaCl, 50mM Tris-HCl, 0.2% SDS, 1mM DTT). 60µl of 25% Triton X-100 was added to each sample and sonicated briefly for 10 seconds until clear. The lysate was centrifuged at 15,000 x g for 10 minutes at 4°C and the supernatants were collected. The protein concentration of each sample was measured by Bradford assay and normalized using 50mM Tris-HCl.  Streptavidin magnetic Dynabeads (Life Technologies, Carlsbad, CA) were washed with 50mM Tris-HCl two times. An equal volume of beads was added to each sample, ensuring that 30-50µl of beads were used per mg of protein. The bead-sample mixture was rotated end-over-end overnight at 4°C, after which the protein-coated beads were washed twice with 2% SDS using gentle rotation for 5 minutes. The beads were washed in this way again with wash buffer (0.1% Na-DOC, 1% Triton X-100, 0.5M NaCl, 50mM HEPES pH 7.5, 1µM EDTA), followed by (0.5% Na-DOC, 250uM LiCl, 0.5% NP-40, 10mM Tris-HCl, 1µM EDTA). The beads were washed once with 50mM Tris-HCl using gentle rotation for 5 minutes and eluted with 40µl of elution buffer (1x LDS, 0.02M DTT, 4mM Biotin) for 20 minutes at 98°C. This elution step was repeated one more time and the final eluate was collected.  Sample preparation was performed as described in the autophosphorylation methods section. The resulting peptide mixture was analyzed on a 1D nanoLC-MS/MS platform using a standardized 140 m method. Peptides were separated on an C18-AQ column (75 μm × 50 cm, Reprosil-Pur C18-AQ, 1.9 μm) at 250 nL/min.  The mass spectrometer was a QExactive HF (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Waltham, MA) set to acquire data in a data-dependent fashion selecting and fragmenting the 10 most intense precursor ions in the ion trap where the exclusion window was set at 60 seconds and multiple charge states of the same ion were allowed.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nRattus norvegicus (rat)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
